### How does filter work internally?

filter accepts a Predicate and returns a new Stream containing only matching elements. It is lazy, stateless, and processes one element at a time.

### What Predicate does filter accept?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Objects;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class FilterPredicateDemo {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true)
        );

        // Lambda predicate:
        accounts.stream().filter(a -> a.active()).toList();

        // Method reference:
        accounts.stream().filter(Account::active).toList();

        // Predicate<? super Account> accepts parent types:
        Predicate<Object> notNull = Objects::nonNull;
        accounts.stream().filter(notNull).toList(); // OK: Object is super of Account
    }
}
```

### How does Predicate composition work?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.function.Predicate;

public class PredicateComposition {
    record Account(String id, BigDecimal balance, boolean active) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("125000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2002", new BigDecimal("8000"), false),
            new Account("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("250000"), true),
            new Account("SAV2003", new BigDecimal("50000"), true)
        );

        Predicate<Account> isActive = Account::active;
        Predicate<Account> isLarge = a -> a.balance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0;

        // Composition:
        List<Account> activeLarge = accounts.stream()
            .filter(isActive.and(isLarge))   // AND
            .toList();

        List<Account> activeOrLarge = accounts.stream()
            .filter(isActive.or(isLarge))    // OR
            .toList();

        List<Account> inactive = accounts.stream()
            .filter(isActive.negate())        // NOT
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Active AND large: " + activeLarge.size());
        System.out.println("Active OR large: " + activeOrLarge.size());
        System.out.println("Inactive: " + inactive.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Active AND large: 2
Active OR large: 3
Inactive: 1
```

### How does the Spliterator drive filter execution?

The Spliterator is the low-level engine that traverses stream elements. For sequential streams, `tryAdvance(Consumer)` pulls one element at a time. For each element, `predicate.test(element)` is called. If true, the element passes downstream. If false, it is dropped and the next element is pulled.

For parallel streams, `trySplit()` divides the source into partitions. Each partition's Spliterator independently tests elements against the predicate, and results are merged.

### What is the difference between layered filters and composed predicates?

```java
// Layered: three separate filter calls
accounts.stream()
    .filter(Account::active)
    .filter(a -> a.balance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0)
    .filter(a -> a.id().startsWith("SAV"))
    .toList();

// Composed: one filter call with composed predicate
Predicate<Account> combined = Account::active
    .and(a -> a.balance().compareTo(new BigDecimal("100000")) > 0)
    .and(a -> a.id().startsWith("SAV"));
accounts.stream().filter(combined).toList();
```

Both produce identical results because stream fusion processes each element through all stages in a single pass. The layered approach is better for debugging. The composed approach is better for unit testing the predicate in isolation.

### What are common mistakes?

Forgetting that filter returns a new Stream and does not modify the original collection. Using filter for side effects (modifying external state inside the predicate). Not providing proper hashCode/equals when using distinct after filter.

### What should a senior engineer know?

Named predicates stored as static factory methods in a FilterRules class are reusable across different streams and independently testable. Predicate.not() (Java 11) replaces the verbose `Predicate.not(Account::isDormant)` pattern. filter is the most commonly used intermediate operation and is the first thing to place in a pipeline to reduce the dataset early.
